WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.200
We're definitely living in a golden time for memory research

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:06.420
We're really fortunate because we're really standing on the shoulders of giants

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.940
But unlike these sort of real pioneers

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.060
Lashley, Hebb, even Kandel

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.900
we now have some tools available

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.180
that allows us to really probe the brain in new ways.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.980
I think it's very tempting to think of memory

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.080
as a very sort of high fidelity record of events we experience

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.240
but there's so much like a video recording

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.740
but it turns out that's not the case.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.400
All these things make us what we are

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.300
these interesting sort of bits of knowledge we have

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.140
are the aspects of life which we're interested in

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.240
but to some extent they are not that useful

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.680
but and it may be if you remember too much of that stuff

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.620
it tells me they'll get in the way of having an efficient memory system.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.160
So it's not as if we're coming up with incredibly new theories

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.960
but what we're doing is we're testing some age-old theories that have been out there

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.720
and we can actually test them now using a pretty cool scientific methods.

